{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Who and who are those who go.</b> There are several difficulties in this matter. First, the doubling of ״מִי וָמִי״ — for one would suffice. Furthermore, ״הַהוֹלְכִים״ is in the present tense and does not fit, for he should have said ״מִי יֵלֵךְ״, which is future; for ״הַהוֹלְכִים״ implies that they are going now, and they have not yet gone. Moreover, there are two occurrences of ״נֵלֵךְ״, and surely one of them is superfluous, for he should have said ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ בְּבָנֵינוּ וּבִבְנוֹתֵינוּ נֵלֵךְ״. Furthermore, ״כִּי חַג ה׳ לָנוּ״ — but above he said in the name of HaShem, ״וְיָחֹגּוּ לִי בַּמִּדְבָּר״ (Exodus 5:1), implying that it would be a festival for HaShem and not for us. And this distinction is well known: a festival for HaShem is in the service of HaShem, blessed be He, while a festival for us is in the joy of those who serve, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:8) ״עֲצֶרֶת לַה׳״, and it is written (Numbers 29:35) ״עֲצֶרֶת תִּהְיֶה לָכֶם״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Pesachim 68a) by way of interpretation: ״Half for HaShem and half for you.״ And here, who is wise and who understands the interpretation of the matter? Moreover, it is difficult: ״כִּי אוֹתָהּ אַתֶּם מְבַקְשִׁים״ is in the present tense, and he should have said ״כִּי אוֹתָהּ בִּקַּשְׁתֶּם״ in the past tense, for now they are seeking more than this, since they said ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ״ etc. — behold, they sought to uproot everything from there. And the explanation of the commentators is already well known, and in place of the word ״אוֹתָהּ״ he should have said ״זֶה אַתֶּם מְבַקְשִׁים״." "And the resolution of all these difficulties is that Pharaoh did not ask Moses who would go; rather, he decreed as a certainty that they would certainly not go except for the males, whose custom it is to slaughter. And he said, ״לְכוּ עִבְדוּ אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״, and roam to the far corners of the world — go out and see what the people do: who and who is it whose way it is to go. Therefore he said the doubling ״מִי וָמִי״, that is, whether you turn right or whether you turn left to see in every place what is customary — who is it whose way it is to go to slaughter. And with this, the phrase ״הַהוֹלְכִים״ is reconciled, which refers to all those who go throughout the entire world, and from there you will see and so shall you also do according to the custom. And Moses answered, ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ נֵלֵךְ״ — according to what appears, and according to its plain sense, ״נֵלֵךְ״ is entirely superfluous; that is, both of them are superfluous, since it refers to the question of ״מִי וָמִי הַהוֹלְכִים״, and he should have answered ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ, בְּבָנֵינוּ״ etc., and it would all refer to the goers. Rather, it certainly does not refer to the goers, and he means to say: You tell us to go according to the way of those who go throughout the world, but we shall not do so; rather, ״בִּנְעָרֵינוּ וּבִזְקֵנֵינוּ נֵלֵךְ״. And the difference between ״נֵלֵךְ״ and ״הַהוֹלְכִים״ is that these are not brought close to one another. For even among all those who go, no one goes to slaughter except those whose way it is to slaughter; nevertheless, we shall go, each and every one. And he gave a reason for the matter: ״כִּי חַג ה׳ לָנוּ״ — for in truth, among all those who go, since they go only to slaughter, it is fitting that none should go except those whose way it is to slaughter. But we, besides the service — that is, a festival for HaShem — have yet another commandment upon us, and that is the joy of the festival. This is what he said: ״כִּי חַג ה׳ לָנוּ״ — it must also be ״לָנוּ״. And it is well known that a person's joy is not complete except when his wife and children and all his possessions are with him, for how can he sing the song of HaShem on foreign soil while his wife and children and possessions are held as pledge for him? And one who dwells without a wife dwells without joy. Therefore he said ״נֵלֵךְ״ twice, to make two divisions among those who go: ״נְעָרֵינוּ וּזְקֵנֵינוּ נֵלֵךְ״ to serve, and ״בָנֵינוּ וּבְנוֹתֵינוּ״ shall go for the sake of joy — that is, that we may rejoice together with them, to fulfill the commandment of HaShem upon us to give half to HaShem and half to us and to our children, for the sake of joy."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:14.792304479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}